      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": "The effect of the endogenous opioid peptides, methionine-enkephalin (Met-ENK), beta-endorphin (beta-END) and dynorphin-(1-17) (DYN) on the aversive behavior produced by intrathecal (i.t.) administration of substance P (SP) was studied in mice. A low dose of i.t. administered Met-ENK gave a marked reduction of the SP-induced response. In the tail-flick assay, such doses of Met-ENK were ineffective in producing antinociception. At much higher doses, however, Met-ENK obtained antinociceptive activity. In contrast, beta-END and DYN had about the same potency in inhibiting the SP-induced behavioural response and in the tail-flick test, respectively. These results suggest that opioid peptides, particularly enkephalin neurons in the spinal cord influence SP-induced aversive behaviour."},
